---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Film from '../../components/Film.astro';
import Status from '../../components/Status.astro';
import { url } from '../../data/url';
const services = [
  ['Google Places', 'Finds candidate places near a point; live opening hours, ratings, photos.', 'Every plan', 'Place IDs only. Details are fetched fresh, never copied into our database.'],
  ['Google Routes: matrix', 'Travel time between every pair of the shortlisted places and home, with traffic.', 'Every plan with stops', 'Not stored. Times change by the hour.'],
  ['Google Routes: route', 'The actual road path for the chosen plan, to draw on the map.', 'Once, for the chosen plan', 'Not stored.'],
  ['Google Geocoding', 'Turns “near Besant Nagar” or a typed address into a point on the map.', 'When a place is named', 'Not stored beyond the conversation.'],
  ['Weather', 'Rain or heat this evening, so an outdoor plan can be swapped.', 'Plans that include outdoors', 'Not stored.'],
  ['Google Maps SDK', 'The map inside the app: pins, the line, the timeline.', 'Showing a plan', 'Nothing.'],
  ['Claude (the AI)', 'Understands the sentence; writes the short reasons at the end.', 'Twice per reply', 'We keep the conversation card, not the raw call.'],
  ['Our database', 'The notebook for each person; our own facts: veg confirmed, cost bands, seats, clubs.', 'Every plan', 'Ours. This is the moat.'],
  ['Organisers and partners', 'Events, clubs, seats and offers we can’t get from Google.', 'When an event fits', 'Ours, with a source and a date.'],
  ['Push notifications', 'The Thursday nudge, the re-check before leaving, the recap.', 'A few times a week', 'Device token only.'],
];
---
<Base slug="outside/" title="What we rely on outside" description="Which outside services The Cousin uses, what each does, and how asking, recommending, narrowing, routing and the map work.">
  <header class="wrap phead">
    <p class="chapter">03b · What we rely on outside</p>
    <h1 class="h1" data-split="lines" data-intro>Google knows the city. We know the person. Code decides.</h1>
    <div class="headrow" data-reveal="up" data-intro data-delay="0.3">
      <p class="lede muted">Our algorithms don’t work alone. They lean on a few outside services for maps, live hours and travel times, and on an AI model for language. None of them is asked to choose the plan.</p>
      <Status s="designed" />
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Watch one reply</p>
    <Film id="film-outside" name="outside" title="Film · Who is called, in what order" length="21 s" label="Sequence diagram: you, our app, Claude, Google and our data as columns; twelve steps of one reply flow between them" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">The services</p>
    <div class="tbl" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>What it gives us</th><th>When it’s called</th><th>What we keep</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{services.map(([a, b, c, d]) => <tr><td><b>{a}</b></td><td>{b}</td><td>{c}</td><td>{d}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Google’s rules</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Google’s terms let us keep place IDs indefinitely, but not copies of names, hours, ratings or photos beyond a short cache. So our database stores the ID plus our own facts (veg confirmed by the place, cost band, seats, clubs), and fetches the rest fresh when a plan is made. Results must be shown on a Google map, with attribution.</p>
      <p class="muted">Rough cost per reply: about two AI calls and a handful of Google calls. Searching only near the person, and only sending 12 places to the travel-time matrix, keeps this small.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">Five questions, answered</p>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="qa">
        <h2>How does it ask counter-questions?</h2>
        <p>It doesn’t ask by default. After the AI cuts the sentence into pieces, code checks three gates: can a missing piece be filled safely from the notebook or today; would the answer change the picks (budget for 10 people does; preferred table side doesn’t); and is a wrong guess expensive. Only if it can’t be filled, would change the picks, and is costly to get wrong does it ask, and then only one short question per reply, with tap-able answers. Otherwise it plans with a stated guess: “Assuming medium spice.” <a href={url('algorithms/asking/')}>More on asking</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <h2>How does it know what to recommend?</h2>
        <p>Three sources, mixed by code. The notebook: what this person loved, liked or skipped before, and their taste seed from day one. Today: what they said in this conversation, which wins for tonight. And the place’s own facts. Magnets score each allowed place; then a mix keeps a safe pick, a stretch and sometimes a wild card, so the list isn’t three copies of the same idea. <a href={url('algorithms/fences-and-magnets/')}>Fences and magnets</a>, <a href={url('algorithms/mix-and-variety/')}>mix and variety</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <h2>How does it narrow down?</h2>
        <p>Area first, then kind, then fences. Google Places is asked only for the right kind of place within reach of home and the deadline. It returns up to 20 places per call, so we run a few searches by kind and area and merge them with our own listings: a few hundred candidates. Then every fence runs in code: veg, budget, seats, open on arrival, home by 10. About 40 survive. Ranking keeps the best 6 dinners and 6 desserts. Only those 12 go on to routing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <h2>How does the route order work?</h2>
        <p>One call to the Routes matrix gives travel times between home and all 12 places, with traffic: a 13 by 13 table. The solver then tries every dinner with every dessert (36 pairs), in the order the evening needs: dinner first, dessert on the way home. It drops any pair that misses 10 pm with a 15 minute buffer, and prefers the arc that feels right: a short first hop, the main thing in the middle, a gentle end. <a href={url('algorithms/route-and-arc/')}>Route and arc</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="qa">
        <h2>How is it placed on the map?</h2>
        <p>For the chosen plan only, Routes returns the road path as a line. The app draws it on a Google map with numbered pins and a timeline underneath: leave 7:15, dinner 7:30, dessert 9:05, home 9:40. Before leaving, hours and traffic are checked again, and the plan updates if something changed.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap row">
    <p class="chapter">For engineers</p>
    <details class="eng">
      <summary>Calls per reply and fallbacks</summary>
      <div class="prose">
        <pre class="code">1  Claude      parse sentence → pieces (JSON with types)
2  Places      searchNearby(kind, centre, radius) × a few   ≤ 20 each
3  our DB      fences on our facts; magnets on notebook
4  Routes      computeRouteMatrix(13 × 13, traffic)
5  solver      36 pairs → best feasible plan
6  Routes      computeRoutes(chosen) → polyline
7  Claude      reasons around facts printed by code</pre>
        <p>If Google is slow or down, we fall back to cached travel estimates by area and say “times are estimates”. If the AI is down, the plan is still made; reasons fall back to templates.</p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>
</Base>
